          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Is I10 a complete code, or does it need a fourth character?",
          "answer": "**No, I10 is a complete code and does not need a fourth character.** It is three characters long, and no further characters can be added to it.\n\nThe coding guidance says I10 is unusual in this respect. Most ICD-10-CM categories need a fourth, fifth or sixth character before the code is complete. I10 takes none, so asking to \"add more specificity\" to I10 cannot be done within the classification. The guidance also notes that high blood pressure described as arterial, essential, primary or systemic all map to this same code. (Source: *Coding hypertension: I10 and the hypertensive disease family*.)\n\nThe general rule comes from the ICD-10-CM structure document. Codes run from three to seven characters, and a decimal point follows the first three characters when more follow. That is why I10 has no decimal point, while a code such as E11.9 does. The three-character form is the category, and later characters add detail. (Source: *ICD-10-CM code structure and the conventions that govern selection*.)\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
